WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.710 line:85%
There’s a bottle in the fridge if you need it.

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.963 line:85%
You’ve just arrived for an overnight shift,

3
00:00:08.993 --> 00:00:11.645 line:85%
and that is the first thing the parent tells you.

4
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:16.554 line:85%
You could nod, put your bag away, and assume the bottle is for the next feed.

5
00:00:17.184 --> 00:00:20.418 line:85%
But that one sentence leaves several decisions open.

6
00:00:21.398 --> 00:00:23.992 line:85%
Do they want you to bring the baby to them to nurse first?

7
00:00:24.972 --> 00:00:27.971 line:85%
Is this bottle intended for this baby and this shift?

8
00:00:28.951 --> 00:00:32.015 line:85%
When do they want to hear from you if a feed goes differently?

9
00:00:32.995 --> 00:00:36.975 line:85%
Tonight we’ll follow that same shift from arrival to morning handoff.

10
00:00:37.605 --> 00:00:41.106 line:85%
You’ll hear what to ask, watch a bottle feeding demonstration,

11
00:00:41.136 --> 00:00:45.319 line:85%
and work through two timelines that look similar but have different rules.

12
00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:47.477 line:85%
Keep the worksheet nearby.

13
00:00:48.107 --> 00:00:52.045 line:85%
You’ll use it to write a read back and a parent update before the quiz.

14
00:00:53.025 --> 00:00:57.098 line:85%
A useful feeding plan tells you what to do when things go as expected,

15
00:00:57.128 --> 00:00:59.501 line:85%
and how to reach someone when they don’t.

16
00:01:00.131 --> 00:01:02.661 line:85%
The examples in this workshop are fictional.

17
00:01:03.291 --> 00:01:06.821 line:85%
The times and amounts you’ll see are details to document,

18
00:01:06.851 --> 00:01:09.256 line:85%
never targets to apply to another baby.

19
00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:11.635 line:85%
Your first practice.

20
00:01:12.265 --> 00:01:15.755 line:85%
Write one question you would ask that parent before they go to bed.

21
00:01:35.785 --> 00:01:37.974 line:85%
That one sentence could point two ways.

22
00:01:38.604 --> 00:01:40.601 line:85%
Bring the baby to the parent to nurse.

23
00:01:41.231 --> 00:01:43.953 line:85%
Or offer a bottle, if that is what you agreed.

24
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:47.100 line:85%
Here is one question that settles it.

25
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.904 line:85%
When baby is ready, would you like me to bring them to you first,

26
00:01:51.934 --> 00:01:55.147 line:85%
and under what circumstances should I use this bottle?

27
00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:58.651 line:85%
When was the last feed?

28
00:01:59.281 --> 00:02:02.003 line:85%
What would you like me to do when the baby cues again?

29
00:02:02.933 --> 00:02:04.354 line:85%
They nursed around eight.

30
00:02:04.984 --> 00:02:06.533 line:85%
Please bring them to me first.

31
00:02:07.163 --> 00:02:09.245 line:85%
The bottle is backup if I ask for it.

32
00:02:10.175 --> 00:02:13.430 line:85%
Which bottle is it, and when was it prepared or stored?

33
00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.658 line:85%
It’s the bottle labeled expressed milk, with today’s date.

34
00:02:18.288 --> 00:02:20.285 line:85%
Let’s check its storage time together.

35
00:02:21.215 --> 00:02:25.755 line:85%
If the baby takes less than usual, or the feed doesn’t go as expected,

36
00:02:25.785 --> 00:02:27.649 line:85%
should I wake you right away?

37
00:02:28.279 --> 00:02:29.955 line:85%
What’s the best way to reach you?

38
00:02:30.885 --> 00:02:32.413 line:85%
Yes, knock on my door.

39
00:02:33.043 --> 00:02:35.999 line:85%
If you’re unsure which bottle to use, ask me first.

40
00:02:36.929 --> 00:02:41.761 line:85%
Notice how each question removes a decision you otherwise might have had to make

41
00:02:41.791 --> 00:02:42.126 line:85%
alone.

42
00:02:43.106 --> 00:02:44.783 line:85%
Start with what happened last.

43
00:02:45.413 --> 00:02:47.815 line:85%
Mark approximate times as approximate.

44
00:02:48.795 --> 00:02:51.419 line:85%
Next, agree on the plan for the next feed,

45
00:02:51.449 --> 00:02:55.211 line:85%
including whether the parent wants to nurse, offer a bottle,

46
00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:58.877 line:85%
or use another arrangement already agreed with the family.

47
00:02:59.857 --> 00:03:04.435 line:85%
If there are two bottles, or more than one baby, confirm the intended bottle.

48
00:03:05.415 --> 00:03:07.929 line:85%
Ask about written clinician instructions,

49
00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:10.846 line:85%
and resolve discrepancies before you take over.

50
00:03:11.476 --> 00:03:16.742 line:85%
If the family says a clinician gave a specific plan you can’t verify or carry out,

51
00:03:16.772 --> 00:03:20.875 line:85%
involve the parent and ask them to clarify with their care team.

52
00:03:21.855 --> 00:03:23.895 line:85%
Then read the plan back in your own words.

53
00:03:24.825 --> 00:03:26.373 line:85%
Bring baby to you first.

54
00:03:27.003 --> 00:03:29.768 line:85%
Use the confirmed backup only if you ask.

55
00:03:30.398 --> 00:03:35.083 line:85%
Knock if the baby takes less than usual, or the bottle details aren’t clear.

56
00:03:36.013 --> 00:03:40.441 line:85%
A read back gives the parent a chance to correct you while they are still available.

57
00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:42.821 line:85%
Now a different family.

58
00:03:43.451 --> 00:03:45.234 line:85%
At handoff, this parent says:

59
00:03:46.164 --> 00:03:48.054 line:85%
Only wake me if it’s important.

60
00:03:48.984 --> 00:03:50.533 line:85%
What counts as important?

61
00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:54.247 line:85%
Write an observable trigger, and the way you will reach them.

62
00:04:24.277 --> 00:04:25.677 line:85%
Here is one example.

63
00:04:26.607 --> 00:04:29.555 line:85%
If the baby repeatedly takes less than usual,

64
00:04:29.585 --> 00:04:34.321 line:85%
or I can’t confirm the bottle’s storage history, should I knock or call?

65
00:04:35.251 --> 00:04:38.698 line:85%
It names what you will watch for and how you will reach them,

66
00:04:38.728 --> 00:04:42.346 line:85%
so the parent can agree or change it while they are still awake.

67
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.537 line:85%
Start with clean hands and clean feeding supplies.

68
00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:51.678 line:85%
Check the label against the plan before you bring the bottle over.

69
00:04:52.658 --> 00:04:57.087 line:85%
In this demonstration, the parent has asked us to offer the confirmed bottle.

70
00:04:57.717 --> 00:05:01.676 line:85%
Watch how the caregiver holds the baby close, and keeps hold of the bottle.

71
00:05:02.656 --> 00:05:05.549 line:85%
The bottle is angled, rather than held vertically.

72
00:05:06.179 --> 00:05:08.943 line:85%
That gives the baby more control over the feed.

73
00:05:09.923 --> 00:05:13.324 line:85%
This is a demonstration of routine responsive feeding,

74
00:05:13.354 --> 00:05:18.469 line:85%
so a baby with a clinician directed feeding plan may need different instructions.

75
00:05:19.449 --> 00:05:21.489 line:85%
Now watch the caregiver slow down.

76
00:05:22.119 --> 00:05:24.969 line:85%
When the baby wants a break, make room for that pause.

77
00:05:25.599 --> 00:05:28.705 line:85%
In real life, you’re watching the baby throughout the feed.

78
00:05:29.685 --> 00:05:34.371 line:85%
If the baby closes their mouth, turns away, or keeps showing that they are done,

79
00:05:34.401 --> 00:05:35.137 line:85%
end the feed.

80
00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:39.257 line:85%
How much is left in the bottle is information for the log.

81
00:05:39.887 --> 00:05:41.820 line:85%
It does not tell you to keep feeding.

82
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.330 line:85%
Here is the label check and the clean setup.

83
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.111 line:85%
The caregiver keeps the baby close and remains with them throughout the feed.

84
00:06:06.141 --> 00:06:07.349 line:85%
Here is the pause.

85
00:06:07.979 --> 00:06:11.021 line:85%
Notice that the caregiver is watching the baby’s face,

86
00:06:11.051 --> 00:06:12.727 line:85%
and allowing the baby a break.

87
00:06:28.757 --> 00:06:30.434 line:85%
Here is the end of the feed.

88
00:06:31.064 --> 00:06:33.604 line:85%
The caregiver records the amount if measured,

89
00:06:33.634 --> 00:06:36.687 line:85%
and checks the correct handling rule for what is left.

90
00:06:52.717 --> 00:06:53.647 line:85%
Your turn.

91
00:06:54.277 --> 00:06:57.575 line:85%
The baby turns away twice, and keeps their mouth closed.

92
00:06:58.205 --> 00:07:00.031 line:85%
Pause, stop, or continue?

93
00:07:20.061 --> 00:07:22.869 line:85%
Stop when the baby continues to signal fullness.

94
00:07:23.499 --> 00:07:28.253 line:85%
If the amount differs from what the parent expected, record what you observed,

95
00:07:28.283 --> 00:07:30.338 line:85%
and use the agreed call back plan.

96
00:07:30.968 --> 00:07:34.095 line:85%
Never prop the bottle, or pressure the baby to finish.

97
00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:37.943 line:85%
Here are two capped bottles.

98
00:07:38.573 --> 00:07:40.335 line:85%
One holds prepared formula.

99
00:07:40.965 --> 00:07:43.047 line:85%
The other holds expressed breast milk.

100
00:07:43.677 --> 00:07:45.225 line:85%
Start with the type of milk.

101
00:07:45.855 --> 00:07:48.065 line:85%
Then ask which event started the clock.

102
00:07:49.045 --> 00:07:52.056 line:85%
For prepared infant formula at room temperature,

103
00:07:52.086 --> 00:07:57.186 line:85%
use it within two hours of preparation, and within one hour of starting the feed.

104
00:07:57.816 --> 00:07:59.087 line:85%
Both limits matter.

105
00:08:00.067 --> 00:08:05.430 line:85%
If freshly prepared formula won’t be used promptly, refrigerate it right away

106
00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:10.192 line:85%
and use it within twenty four hours, following CDC storage guidance.

107
00:08:11.172 --> 00:08:15.323 line:85%
When a formula feed ends, discard what the baby left in that bottle.

108
00:08:16.303 --> 00:08:17.873 line:85%
Here’s the first timeline.

109
00:08:18.503 --> 00:08:20.671 line:85%
The formula is prepared at 10:00 p.m.

110
00:08:21.301 --> 00:08:23.767 line:85%
The baby starts feeding at 10:30 p.m.

111
00:08:24.397 --> 00:08:26.756 line:85%
The feed ends at 10:45 p.m.

112
00:08:27.386 --> 00:08:28.913 line:85%
There’s some formula left.

113
00:08:29.893 --> 00:08:31.719 line:85%
When should you discard what’s left?

114
00:08:56.749 --> 00:09:00.154 line:85%
Discard it when the feed ends, at 10:45 p.m.

115
00:09:00.784 --> 00:09:05.193 line:85%
The one hour window from the start of the feed does not turn leftovers into a

116
00:09:05.223 --> 00:09:06.173 line:85%
bottle for later.

117
00:09:06.803 --> 00:09:10.549 line:85%
Write the event that controls the decision, not just the time.

118
00:09:11.529 --> 00:09:14.059 line:85%
Now change the label to expressed breast milk.

119
00:09:15.039 --> 00:09:18.831 line:85%
CDC says to use it within two hours after it is warmed

120
00:09:18.861 --> 00:09:21.025 line:85%
or brought to room temperature.

121
00:09:21.655 --> 00:09:24.323 line:85%
If there’s expressed milk left after a feed,

122
00:09:24.353 --> 00:09:27.022 line:85%
use it within two hours after the feed ends,

123
00:09:27.052 --> 00:09:29.966 line:85%
while respecting any earlier limit that applies.

124
00:09:30.596 --> 00:09:34.897 line:85%
Warming or feeding does not restart a deadline that already applies.

125
00:09:35.877 --> 00:09:38.279 line:85%
This bottle was warmed at 11:00 p.m.

126
00:09:38.909 --> 00:09:41.268 line:85%
That starts a warming limit of 1:00 a.m.

127
00:09:42.248 --> 00:09:44.650 line:85%
The feed ends at 11:30 p.m.

128
00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.258 line:85%
That starts a leftover limit of 1:30 a.m.

129
00:09:49.238 --> 00:09:50.808 line:85%
Which deadline comes first?

130
00:09:51.788 --> 00:09:55.065 line:85%
For this case, assume no earlier storage limit applies.

131
00:10:25.095 --> 00:10:26.686 line:85%
1:00 a.m. comes first.

132
00:10:27.316 --> 00:10:29.462 line:85%
Two different events started those clocks.

133
00:10:30.092 --> 00:10:33.945 line:85%
You don’t get to extend the warming limit because the feed ended later.

134
00:10:34.925 --> 00:10:35.769 line:85%
One more.

135
00:10:36.399 --> 00:10:39.612 line:85%
These two bottles look the same, but one has no label.

136
00:10:54.642 --> 00:10:58.175 line:85%
You can’t confirm what’s in this bottle, or how long it’s been out.

137
00:10:58.805 --> 00:11:04.044 line:85%
Set it aside, check with the parent, and use a verified bottle from the agreed plan.

138
00:11:04.674 --> 00:11:06.628 line:85%
Don’t guess by looking at the liquid.

139
00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:13.118 line:85%
If the plan is direct nursing, bring the baby to the parent when agreed.

140
00:11:13.748 --> 00:11:17.259 line:85%
You can help with practical tasks, like getting a burp cloth

141
00:11:17.289 --> 00:11:19.030 line:85%
or handling the diaper change.

142
00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:21.900 line:85%
Record the session as observed.

143
00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:25.188 line:85%
Don’t estimate ounces from a nursing session.

144
00:11:26.168 --> 00:11:30.577 line:85%
If the parent asks about ongoing latch or milk transfer concerns,

145
00:11:30.607 --> 00:11:32.899 line:85%
a lactation professional can help,

146
00:11:32.929 --> 00:11:38.362 line:85%
and the pediatric team can assess concerns about the baby’s intake or hydration.

147
00:11:39.342 --> 00:11:42.149 line:85%
A useful update begins with what you observed.

148
00:11:42.779 --> 00:11:47.012 line:85%
Give the time, milk type, measured amount if known, the baby’s response,

149
00:11:47.042 --> 00:11:48.019 line:85%
and what you did.

150
00:11:48.999 --> 00:11:52.681 line:85%
Ask the parent what they want you to do next within the agreed plan.

151
00:11:53.311 --> 00:11:55.542 line:85%
Avoid labels such as “bad feeder.”

152
00:11:56.522 --> 00:11:59.521 line:85%
In the morning, carry forward any open question,

153
00:11:59.551 --> 00:12:04.065 line:85%
so the next caregiver knows who was contacted and what follow up is due.

154
00:12:05.045 --> 00:12:09.137 line:85%
If feeding is repeatedly different from the baby’s usual pattern,

155
00:12:09.167 --> 00:12:13.892 line:85%
the baby is having trouble feeding, or wet diapers are fewer than expected,

156
00:12:13.922 --> 00:12:18.647 line:85%
tell the parent promptly and help them reach the pediatrician for guidance.

157
00:12:19.627 --> 00:12:25.161 line:85%
For severe trouble breathing, unresponsiveness, or blue or gray lips or face,

158
00:12:25.191 --> 00:12:30.797 line:85%
call 911 immediately in the United States, and follow dispatcher instructions.

159
00:12:31.777 --> 00:12:32.707 line:85%
Practice.

160
00:12:33.337 --> 00:12:35.889 line:85%
A shift note says, “Baby barely ate.”

161
00:12:36.519 --> 00:12:38.985 line:85%
Rewrite it using measured observations.

162
00:13:04.015 --> 00:13:05.670 line:85%
Here is a factual version.

163
00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:07.743 line:85%
12:10 a.m.

164
00:13:08.373 --> 00:13:11.735 line:85%
Expressed milk, 2 oz offered, 1 oz taken.

165
00:13:12.365 --> 00:13:13.509 line:85%
Turned away twice.

166
00:13:14.139 --> 00:13:16.583 line:85%
Stopped and notified parent as agreed.

167
00:13:17.513 --> 00:13:20.022 line:85%
Each detail is something you saw or measured.

168
00:13:20.652 --> 00:13:25.296 line:85%
The parent can compare it with the baby’s usual feeds and decide on follow up,

169
00:13:25.326 --> 00:13:27.513 line:85%
without guessing what “barely” meant.

170
00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.374 line:85%
Let’s put the pieces together.

171
00:13:32.354 --> 00:13:36.463 line:85%
At arrival the parent says, “There’s a bottle in the fridge if you need it.”

172
00:13:37.093 --> 00:13:40.583 line:85%
They clarify that the baby should come to them to nurse first.

173
00:13:41.213 --> 00:13:45.108 line:85%
Later, they ask you to use a confirmed bottle of expressed milk.

174
00:13:46.088 --> 00:13:48.533 line:85%
At 12:10 a.m. you offer 2 oz.

175
00:13:49.163 --> 00:13:52.077 line:85%
The baby takes 1 oz, and turns away twice.

176
00:13:53.057 --> 00:13:57.861 line:85%
This was less than usual, and the parent asked you to wake them for a smaller than

177
00:13:57.891 --> 00:13:58.509 line:85%
usual feed.

178
00:13:59.489 --> 00:14:03.854 line:85%
The bottle was warmed at midnight, and the feed ended at 12:20 a.m.

179
00:14:04.834 --> 00:14:09.436 line:85%
On your worksheet, write one clarification question you could have asked at

180
00:14:09.466 --> 00:14:12.463 line:85%
arrival, a shift note, and a short spoken update.

181
00:14:13.093 --> 00:14:16.459 line:85%
Then work out the earliest deadline for the leftover milk,

182
00:14:16.489 --> 00:14:19.036 line:85%
assuming no other storage limit comes first.

183
00:14:20.016 --> 00:14:22.205 line:85%
A soft chime marks each new task.

184
00:14:22.835 --> 00:14:24.981 line:85%
Pause the video if you need more time.

185
00:16:05.011 --> 00:16:06.623 line:85%
At arrival, you could ask:

186
00:16:07.553 --> 00:16:09.923 line:85%
When do you want me to bring the baby to nurse,

187
00:16:09.953 --> 00:16:12.067 line:85%
and when should I offer the backup bottle?

188
00:16:12.997 --> 00:16:17.776 line:85%
The shift note gives the time, type, amount offered and taken, the cues,

189
00:16:17.806 --> 00:16:21.650 line:85%
and the action: stopped feeding and woke parent as agreed.

190
00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:24.605 line:85%
Your spoken update can be brief.

191
00:16:25.535 --> 00:16:29.324 line:85%
Baby took one ounce of the two offered and turned away twice.

192
00:16:29.954 --> 00:16:32.569 line:85%
I stopped and came to get you like we planned.

193
00:16:33.199 --> 00:16:34.705 line:85%
How would you like to proceed?

194
00:16:35.635 --> 00:16:39.104 line:85%
For the leftover milk, two hours from warming is 2:00 a.m.

195
00:16:39.734 --> 00:16:42.947 line:85%
Two hours from the end of the feed is 2:20 a.m.

196
00:16:43.577 --> 00:16:45.680 line:85%
The earlier one, 2:00 a.m., governs.

197
00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:50.811 line:85%
Document the deadline if the family plans to use the milk within it.

198
00:16:51.441 --> 00:16:54.505 line:85%
The exercise amounts describe this fictional feed.

199
00:16:55.485 --> 00:16:56.927 line:85%
One more look at the note.

200
00:16:57.907 --> 00:16:58.730 line:85%
Time.

201
00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:00.311 line:85%
Milk type.

202
00:17:00.941 --> 00:17:02.597 line:85%
Amount offered and taken.

203
00:17:03.227 --> 00:17:04.135 line:85%
Cues.

204
00:17:04.765 --> 00:17:05.610 line:85%
Action.

205
00:17:06.590 --> 00:17:08.715 line:85%
Warmed at midnight, so 2:00 a.m.

206
00:17:09.345 --> 00:17:12.771 line:85%
Feed ended at 12:20 a.m., so 2:20 a.m.

207
00:17:13.401 --> 00:17:15.141 line:85%
The earlier deadline governs.

208
00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:20.952 line:85%
Before a feed, read the plan back.

209
00:17:21.932 --> 00:17:25.913 line:85%
During the feed, let the baby’s cues guide when to pause and stop.

210
00:17:26.893 --> 00:17:31.193 line:85%
Afterward, apply the right milk handling limit, and report what happened.

211
00:17:32.173 --> 00:17:34.767 line:85%
Keep the worksheet handy as a field reference.

212
00:17:35.397 --> 00:17:40.381 line:85%
Once you’ve completed the required viewing, take the untimed five question quiz.

213
00:17:41.011 --> 00:17:44.245 line:85%
Four correct answers earn your Certificate of Completion.
